# Marketlake: the two pmset writes the control plane needs, and nothing else.
# Neither rule wildcards its argument. sudo joins a command's arguments into one
# string before matching, so a wildcard there spans whitespace and admits what
# follows the wake. Both writes keep reading arguments past the event they set.
# The weekday alarm's argument is fixed, so it is spelled out. The Sunday
# one-shot's date changes weekly, so that rule is an anchored regular
# expression. It pins the date's shape and the wake time, and the closing
# anchor leaves no room for a trailing setting.
# The sleep-disabling write is rejected by design and stays password-gated.
someone ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pmset repeat wakeorpoweron MTWRF 08\:25\:00
someone ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/pmset ^schedule[[:space:]]wakeorpoweron[[:space:]][0-9][0-9]/[0-9][0-9]/[0-9][0-9][[:space:]]19:55:00$
